WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.260
Our species, Homo sapiens, arose in Africa.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.180
Not in one place, and not at one moment.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.820
People with partly modern anatomy lived in north Africa about 300,000 years ago, and

4
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:22.080
in east Africa by at least 233,000 years ago, in populations that split and merged

5
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.120
again. Later, some of their descendants spread across the world, met Neanderthals

6
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.600
and Denisovans, and had children with them.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.980
Pieces of that DNA survive in people today.

8
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.510
Fossil dates need care.

9
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.110
At Jebel Irhoud in Morocco, flints heated by fire lay with the bones, and they give

10
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.530
the date. The face looks like ours.

11
00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.230
The braincase is more elongated.

12
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:52.130
At Omo in Ethiopia, the dated ash lies above the fossil, so the fossil is at least

13
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.250
that old. Ask what was dated, and where it lay.

14
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.813
How do we know about the meetings?

15
00:00:58.793 --> 00:01:03.733
Sampled genomes with ancestry outside Africa share more variants with Neanderthal

16
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:07.013
DNA than sampled African genomes do.

17
00:01:07.653 --> 00:01:10.453
A test called the D statistic measures that surplus.

18
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:13.173
It is not a percentage of ancestry.

19
00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:19.073
On its own it is a clue, not proof, because old structure inside Africa could also

20
00:01:19.073 --> 00:01:22.153
produce it. Stronger evidence comes from time.

21
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:28.767
Every generation, recombination cuts DNA from another population into shorter

22
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:35.087
pieces. Ust'-Ishim was a man in western Siberia who lived about 45,000 years ago.

23
00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:38.407
His Neanderthal segments were longer than ours.

24
00:01:39.287 --> 00:01:44.367
From their length, researchers inferred gene flow 7,000 to 13,000 years before he

25
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:50.867
lived. At Bacho Kiro Cave in Bulgaria, three people had Neanderthal ancestors only a

26
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:51.947
few generations back.

27
00:01:52.917 --> 00:01:58.737
Denisovans are known mostly from DNA, such as a finger bone in Denisova Cave in

28
00:01:58.737 --> 00:02:04.477
Siberia. Another fragment from that cave, Denisova 11, came from a child of a

29
00:02:04.477 --> 00:02:06.757
Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father.

30
00:02:07.657 --> 00:02:11.317
At sites where the two genomes differ, it carried one version of each.

31
00:02:12.037 --> 00:02:14.057
Archaic groups mixed with each other, too.

32
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.790
Some of that inherited DNA still matters.

33
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:24.490
Researchers inferred that sampled Melanesians carry about 4-6% Denisovan ancestry.

34
00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:31.290
In Tibetans, a version of EPAS1, a gene in the low-oxygen response, probably came

35
00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.070
from Denisovans or a related group, and rose because it helped at high altitude.

36
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:38.470
That is adaptive introgression.

37
00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.290
All people alive today are one species.

38
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:47.270
Most common variants are shared worldwide, and genetic variation does not divide

39
00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.790
people into discrete races.
